      "skos:definition": [
        {
          "@value": "In the context of guideline development, the confidence that the estimates of an effect are adequate to support a particular decision or recommendation. Rated high, moderate, low or very low."
        }
      ],
      "skos:scopeNote": "The WHO handbook for guideline development (2nd edition, §9.1, p. 122) names both alternatives: \"Alternative terms to describe the quality of the evidence are 'certainty of the evidence' or 'confidence in the estimates of effect'.\" The handbook uses 'quality of the evidence' 77 times and 'certainty of the evidence' once; the who-guideline-development voice and the platform's `grade-certainty` code list use 'certainty', GRADE's later name. Rated for a body of evidence per outcome, never for a single study. Not the strength of a recommendation, which the handbook keeps apart throughout.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789241548960-eng/sections/sec-153-91-what-is-the-quality-of-the-evidence.md"
